104 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/T (ABSTRACT)
/E (ABSTRACT)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
105 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Short-text topic classification remains a critical challenge in natural language processing, particularly for social media platforms where content is brief, noisy, and context-limited. This work presents a Tweet Topic Classification framework using the AG News corpus as a tweet-like proxy due to its concise headlines and short descriptive text. The objective is to evaluate the performance of classical, deep learning, and transformer-based approaches for accurate topic prediction in short textual inputs.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
106 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Three models were implemented: a TF-IDF + Logistic Regression baseline representing traditional machine learning, a Bidirectional LSTM \(BiLSTM\) model capturing sequential dependencies, and a fine-tuned BERT Base Uncased model leveraging contextualized transformer embeddings. Uniform preprocessing, short-text normalization, and controlled hyperparameter settings were applied to ensure methodological consistency.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
107 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (Experimental analyses demonstrate that the transformer model significantly outperforms the other approaches, achieving a Macro F1-score of 0.9481 and exhibiting strong robustness to limited context. The results highlight the suitability of BERT-based architectures for short-text topic classification and reaffirm the advantages of transformer models in capturing semantic nuances absent in traditional techniques.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
108 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/E (The proposed framework provides a strong foundation for real-world applications including tweet categorization, trend monitoring, automated news filtering, and large-scale social media analytics.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
